                "text": "I'm testing whether the color swap follows a cyclic shift pattern, checking if size ordering of shapes determines which color maps to which in each example, but the cell counts aren't lining up cleanly.\n\nLooking at example 3 with shapes of sizes 8,7,3,6,4, I'm sorting them descending and testing if each shape's color maps to the next shape's color in that sorted sequence, and it seems to fit: shape with 8 cells gets the color of the shape with 7 cells, that one gets the color of the shape with 6 cells, and so on down the chain, wrapping around at the smallest.\n\nChecking ex1's mapping again: 1\u21928, 8\u21925, 5\u21921 works as next-larger-size wraps consistently, but ex3's 8\u21923-cells shape doesn't match my expected wrap to 4-cells. Maybe the rule isn't purely size-based \u2014 perhaps it's tied to the color values themselves rather than shape sizes, since sorting colors 1,5,8 also produces the same cycle by coincidence.\n\nBut testing that same descending-color-cycle idea on ex2 with colors 2,3,8 breaks down, so I'm ruling out pure color-value ordering. Since all shapes in ex2 have equal size (3 cells each), size can't be the deciding factor either \u2014 so I'm now considering that the cycle might instead follow spatial reading order of the shapes' positions.\n\nI check ex2's reading order (2, then 8, then 3) and find the color assignment forms a shift: each shape seems to take the color of the shape before it in reading order. But when I check ex1's reading order (1, 8, 5), the mapping goes the opposite direction \u2014 each shape takes the color of the next one, not the previous. Then in ex3, with five shapes in reading order (6,7,8,3,4), the mapping is inconsistent with either simple forward or backward shift, so I need to look more carefully at what's actually driving the assignment.\n\nI try correlating with cell counts: ex3 shapes have sizes 7,8,4,3,6 respectively, and when I trace the color-swap cycle (7\u21926\u21924\u21928\u21923\u21927), the size sequence is 7,8,4,3,6 \u2014 no obvious pattern jumps out. I try sorting shapes by size instead (3,4,6,7,8 cells) to see if that ordering produces a cleaner cyclic rule.\n\nThat cycle order doesn't match ascending order, so I try another angle: maybe the new color relates to the cell count of each shape rather than sequence position, checking if a shape's size matches another's color number directly in the first example.\n\nLet me try a proximity-based rule instead: maybe each shape takes the color of its nearest neighboring shape. Checking ex3, the 4-block seems closest to the 8-diagonal, matching the observed 4\u21928 swap, so I'm testing whether the 3-block's nearest neighbor is similarly consistent.\n\nBut then in ex1, the 1-zigzag is actually closer to 5-square than to 8-ring, which contradicts the observed 1\u21928 mapping, so nearest-neighbor distance alone doesn't explain it \u2014 I need to look for another ordering principle, maybe based on left-to-right position among the three shapes.\n\nTesting that idea, if I sort shapes by leftmost column and have each shape adopt the color of the previous shape in that order, wrapping around, it fits perfectly across all three examples: ex1's order (1,5,8) works reversed, ex2's order (2,8,3) works, and ex3's order (6,7,3,8,4) matches exactly. This gives me a consistent rule: sort by leftmost column position, then each shape takes on the color of its predecessor in that sequence.\n\nFor the test grid, I identify five shapes\u20149, 8, 4, 5\u2014each with a distinct leftmost column, ordering them as 9,8,4,5 and mapping colors accordingly: 9 becomes 5, 8 becomes 9, 4 becomes 8, and 5 becomes 4. Now I just need to apply these substitutions across the grid.",
